{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791046365,"nonce":"7GRDOa84Sj1lI2wZmd26EomNhrg=","title":"Embed: a host's own scheduler steps a Zyntax async task without blocking","message":"A host with its own event loop (caribou runs Zyntax async calls on its scheduler) cannot use drive_tasks/drive_until: they block the thread until the task settles and sleep on its timers. It needs a per-task step that advances a top-level task as far as it goes without waiting and reports what the task waits on next (its own timer and when it is due, a future a host bridge parked, or nothing), so the host decides when to step it again.\n\nShape: HostTask { step() -\u003e HostTaskStep::{Ready(raw poll word), Timer(deadline), Parked, Yield} } beside drive_until, each step stamped with the task's id and bracketed by its handler-stack segment; a machine parked on a bridge resumed only through resolve_future, never re-polled (the hazard 2af438d describes for drive_until's no-timer branch); dropping an unfinished task tears its parking down as a cancel does. ZyntaxPromise::adopt for a host that called the async entry itself.\n\nReady carries the raw word so a host decodes by the declared type; completion is still read through ab01238's rule (a poll result of 0 is pending). Related: 2af438d, 754e04e.","files":null}]}